  "notes": "The research calls this the most consonant harmony in the whole g-funk section, and that is the entry's reason to exist as a model: major-mode R&B `Imaj7–IV` and `ii–V` cycles where every other LA producer here is in minor. Sevenths carry half the extension mass and `halfStepDissonance` is 0.02, the lowest in this batch. Everything else serves a sung lead: \"low-intensity programming so the sung lead sits forward\" is a lowered accent tier and a soft kick on 1 and the and-of-3, not a removal of lanes, because the record still has tambourine and conga. The bass is documented as round, sustained, melodic and mixed well forward, which is a mid `followRootsProb` with real glide — it moves, but it is not the Bay's free funk line. The wah-wah guitar is the counter-line rather than the lead, so `countermelody.densityRatio` is 0.55 with `answer_lick` weighted over `arp`, and the melody is the layered guitar-and-piano bed at `clean_guitar`. The record was conceived as an R&B duet first, so the arrangement opens on the hook and ends on a long sung outro vamp. ⚠ `medium` per the research's medium-high: the sample source, the layering and the duet framing are documented; the tempo band and the drum numbers are read from the single 1995 record the pair is known for.",
